having been set in " [:a {:href "/results/56034"} "1900"] " by " [:a {:href "/athletes/78599"} "Frank Jarvis"] " (USA) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/79119"} "Walter Tewksbury"] " (USA).  The world mark was 10.6, set in 1906 by Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/76261"} "Knut Lindberg"] ".  Though Lindberg competed in this event, he failed to get by the first round, finishing second in heat eight to " [:a {:href "/athletes/79095"} "Lester Stevens"] " (USA)."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78932"} "James Rector"] " (USA) and Reggie Walker both equaled the Olympic record, Rector doing it in both rounds.  Walker’s record-equaling run came in the final, when he beat Rector by one yard.  At 19 years, 126 days, he set a record, which still stands as of 2024, of being the youngest ever winner of the Olympic 100 metres."] "\n\n" [:p {} "It is often stated that Walker was not actually a member of the South African team in 1908, but travelled to London on his own.  South African Olympic historian Lappe Laubscher has shown that this is not the case, and that he was chosen for the team at the first opportunity.  He notes that Walker was recommended for the team on 1 May 1908, at a meeting of the South African Amateur Athletic and Cycling Union, but he initially could not make the trip because of a lack of funds.  Walker was a native of Natal, and Jim Wallace, a Natal sportswriter, started a campaign to raise funds for Walker’s trip, which was successful."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/75343"} "Reggie Walker"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/RSA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/RSA.png"}] "RSA"]] [:td {} "11.0 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "10.8 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "1